7.1.2 Power Efficiency
----------------------

<1> Orin series module power mode

The Orin series module supports four different power modes known as 15W, 30W, and 50W.
For each mode, several configurations with various CPU and GPU frequencies, and a number of cores online are enabled.
Detailed configuration Orin AGX 64GB module as below:

Mode ID                            0               1               2               3
Power Budget                       EDP             15W             30W             50W
Online CPU                         12              4               8               12
CPU Max Frequency (MHz)            2201.6          1113.6          1728            1497.6
GPU TPC	                           8               3               4               6
GPU Max Frequency (MHz)            1301            408             612             816
DLA cores                          2               2               2               2
DLA Core Max Frequency (MHz)       1600            614.4           1369.6          1369.6
DLA Falcon Max frequency (MHz)     844.8           294.4           729.6           729.6
PVA Cores                          2               1               1               1
PVA VPS Max frequency (MHz)        1369.6          307.2           512             704
PVA AXI Max frequency (MHz)        985.6           217.6           358.4           486.4
Memory Max frequency (MHz)         3200            2133            3200            3200

The default mode is id 2, and Power Budget is 30W.
The EDP represents the upper limitation of Orin series module, so it has no power budget.

<2> Check current power mode

If users want to check the current power mode, please run below command:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# /usr/sbin/nvpmodel -q
NV Power Mode: MODE_30W
2

<2> Change power mode

If users want to change mode of Orin series module, please run below command:
For example, change into mode 0.

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# /usr/sbin/nvpmodel -m 0
NVPM WARN: Golden image context is already created
NVPM WARN: Reboot required for changing to this power mode: 0
NVPM WARN: DO YOU WANT TO REBOOT NOW? enter YES/yes to confirm:
yes
NVPM WARN: rebooting..

Note: The mode persists across power cycles and won't change unless nvpmodel is called again.

7.1.3 Manage CPU State
----------------------

<1> List CPUs

The Orin platform always boot from one CPU denominated the master CPU which cannot be turned off. All other CPUs can be turned off.
For example, the CPUs are listed in the directory /sys/devices/system/cpu/ of Orin AGX platform as below:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# ls -v /sys/devices/system/cpu | grep cpu[0-9]
cpu0
cpu1
cpu2
cpu3
cpu4
cpu5
cpu6
cpu7
cpu8
cpu9
cpu10
cpu11

<2> Check the state of a CPU

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu0/online
1

<3> Turn on a CPU

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# echo 1 > /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu8/online
Detected PIPT I-cache on CPU8
GICv3: CPU8: found redistributor 20000 region 0:0x000000000f540000
CPU8: Booted secondary processor 0x0000020000 [0x410fd421]

<4> Turn off a CPU

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# echo 0 > /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu8/online
psci: CPU8 killed (polled 0 ms)


7.1.4 Change CPU Frequency
--------------------------

<1> To show the CPU lower boundary, upper boundary, and current frequency with below commands:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu1/cpufreq/cpuinfo_min_freq
115200
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu1/cpufreq/cpuinfo_max_freq
2201600
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu1/cpufreq/cpuinfo_cur_freq
729600

<2> Get the available frequencies with below commands:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# cat /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu1/cpufreq/scaling_available_frequencies
115200 192000 268800 345600 422400 499200 576000 652800 729600 806400 883200 960000 1036800 1113600 1190400 1267200 1344000
1420800 1497600 1574400 1651200 1728000 1804800 1881600 1958400 2035200 2112000 2188800 2201600

<3> To change the CPU upper boundary with below commands:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# echo 2112000 > /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu1/cpufreq/scaling_max_freq

<4> To change the CPU lower boundary with below commands:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# echo 192000 > /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu1/cpufreq/scaling_min_freq

<5> To set the static CPU frequency with below commands:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# echo 2201600 > /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu1/cpufreq/scaling_min_freq
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# echo 2201600 > /sys/devices/system/cpu/cpu1/cpufreq/scaling_max_freq

7.1.5 Manage GPU Frequency Scaling
----------------------------------

3D frequency scaling capability allows the NVIDIA Orin platform to change the GPU frequency according to
the load in order to reduce power consumption, 3D frequency scaling is enabled by default.

<1> To disable 3D frequency scaling with below command:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# echo 0 > /sys/devices/platform/bus@0/17000000.gpu/enable_3d_scaling

<2> To enable 3D frequency scaling run the following command:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# echo 1 > /sys/devices/platform/bus@0/17000000.gpu/enable_3d_scaling

7.1.6 Change GPU Frequency
--------------------------

<1> To show the GPU lower boundary, upper boundary, and current frequency with below commands:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# cat /sys/devices/platform/bus@0/17000000.gpu/devfreq/17000000.gpu/min_freq
306000000
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# cat /sys/devices/platform/bus@0/17000000.gpu/devfreq/17000000.gpu/cur_freq
306000000
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# cat /sys/devices/platform/bus@0/17000000.gpu/devfreq/17000000.gpu/max_freq
1300500000

<2> Get the available frequencies with below commands:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# cat /sys/devices/platform/bus@0/17000000.gpu/devfreq/17000000.gpu/available_frequencies
306000000 408000000 510000000 612000000 714000000 816000000 918000000 1020000000 1122000000 1224000000 1300500000

<3> To change the GPU upper boundary with below commands:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# echo 1224000000 > /sys/devices/platform/bus@0/17000000.gpu/devfreq/17000000.gpu/max_freq

<4> To change the GPU lower boundary with below commands:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# echo 408000000 > /sys/devices/platform/bus@0/17000000.gpu/devfreq/17000000.gpu/min_freq

<5> To set the static GPU frequency with below commands:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# echo 1224000000 > /sys/devices/platform/bus@0/17000000.gpu/devfreq/17000000.gpu/min_freq
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# echo 1224000000 > /sys/devices/platform/bus@0/17000000.gpu/devfreq/17000000.gpu/max_freq

7.1.7 Change Memory Frequency
-----------------------------

<1> To show the EMC lower boundary, upper boundary, and current frequency with below commands:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# cat /sys/kernel/debug/bpmp/debug/clk/emc/max_rate
3199000000
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# cat /sys/kernel/debug/bpmp/debug/clk/emc/min_rate
204000000
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# cat /sys/kernel/debug/bpmp/debug/clk/emc/rate
2133000000

<2> Get the available frequencies with below commands:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# ls /sys/kernel/debug/bpmp/debug/emc/tables/regular/
204000  2133000  3199000  665600

<3> To set static EMC frequency with below commands:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/bpmp/debug/clk/emc/mrq_rate_locked
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# echo 1 > /sys/kernel/debug/bpmp/debug/bwmgr/bwmgr_halt
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# echo 2133000000 > /sys/kernel/debug/bpmp/debug/clk/emc/rate

7.1.8 Jetson Clocks Tool
------------------------

NVIDIA provides the jetson_clocks.sh script to maximize Orin module performance by setting static max frequency to CPU, GPU, and EMC clocks.
The script can also be used to show current clock settings, store current clock settings into a file, and restore clock settings from a file.

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# jetson_clocks --help
Maximize jetson performance by setting static max frequency to CPU, GPU and EMC clocks.
Usage:
jetson_clocks [options]
  options,
  --help, -h         show this help message
  --show             display current settings
  --fan              set PWM fan speed to maximal
  --store [file]     store current settings to a file (default: ${HOME}/l4t_dfs.conf)
  --restore [file]   restore saved settings from a file (default: ${HOME}/l4t_dfs.conf)
  run jetson_clocks without any option to set static max frequency to CPU, GPU and EMC clocks.

<1> To show the current settings with below commands:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# jetson_clocks --show

<2> To store the current settings with below commands:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# jetson_clocks --store

<3> To maximize performance with below commands:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# jetson_clocks

<4> To restore the previous settings with below commands:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# jetson_clocks --restore

7.2 System Monitoring Tool
--------------------------

JTOP is a system monitoring tool specifically designed for NVIDIA Orin platform. It provides real-time information about
the platform's resource usage, including CPU, GPU, memory, disk, fan and engine. It also displays hardware temperatures, power consumption and frequency.
JTOP is a user-friendly command-line tool that helps users understand and optimize the performance of applications running on NVIDIA Orin platform.

<1> Run jtop tool

Check the IP address of NVIDIA Orin platform, and then login platform with ssh command:

	$ ssh root@<platform ip>

Set essential environment variable with below command:

	root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# export TERM=xterm-256color

Run "jtop" command:

	root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# jtop

After running the jtop tool, there are some tab items at the bottom of screen.

All Tab: It shows a comprehensive overview of your NVIDIA Orin platform's status and performance.
         It consolidates a wide range of information to give users a complete picture of their system's health
         and resource utilization.
GPU Tab: It shows processes running on the GPU, GPU memory utilization, GPU core utilization, and temperature.
CPU Tab: It shows the utilization and clock frequency of each CPU core.
Memory Tab: It shows memory utilization information and allows adding swap space.
Engine Tab: It shows the status of different hardware accelerator.
Control Tab: It is able to control over the fan, power modes, and jetson_clocks.
INFO Tab: It shows a centralized source of information about current NVIDIA Orin platform.

7.3 CUDA
--------

<1> CUDA Introduction

CUDA is a parallel computing platform that provides a framework for executing computations across thousands of parallel
processing cores within an NVIDIA GPU.
CUDA is a programming model that offers a programming interface for languages like C, C++, and Python, so that developers
could write code that can be executed in parallel on the GPU.

<2> Run CUDA sample

WRlinux has integrated CUDA libs and some samples so that users can develop and run their CUDA applications conveniently.
There are some cuda sample applications in directory /usr/bin/cuda-samples.
For example, user can run the "simplePrintf" application with below commands:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# cd /usr/bin/cuda-samples
root@nvidia-orin-agx:/usr/bin/cuda-samples# ./simplePrintf

GPU Device 0: "Ampere" with compute capability 8.7

Device 0: "Orin" with Compute 8.7 capability
printf() is called. Output:

[3, 0]:         Value is:10
......
[3, 7]:         Value is:10
[1, 0]:         Value is:10
.....
[1, 7]:         Value is:10
[2, 0]:         Value is:10
.....
[2, 7]:         Value is:10
[0, 0]:         Value is:10
.....
[0, 7]:         Value is:10

The simplePrintf sample creates 32 threads running GPU cores parallel.

