7.5 Multimedia
--------------

7.5.1 Camera Preview
--------------------

In below link, NIVDIA lists all the camera module partners.
https://marketplace.nvidia.com/en-us/enterprise/robotics-edge/?category=cameras&page=1&limit=15

For NVIDIA Orin AGX platform:
The arducam 12MP camera module is supported, link as below:
https://www.arducam.com/arducam-12mp-multi-camera-kit-for-nvidia-jetson-agx-orin.html

<1> Connect arducam 12MP camera module with J509 of NVIDIA Orin AGX platform

<2> Deploy camera_overrides.isp file

Download a debian package from arducam 12MP camera release link as below:
https://github.com/ArduCAM/MIPI_Camera/releases/download/v0.0.1-agx-orin/arducam-nvidia-l4t-kernel-t234-agx-orin-5.15.148-tegra-36.4.3-20250224115510_arm64_imx477.deb
And then unpack the debian package with below command:
	$ dpkg-deb -xv <file.deb> <path to extract>
And then copy the camera_overrides.isp from <path to extract>/var/nvidia/nvcam/settings to corresponding directory of NVIDIA Orin AGX board.

Note: If the camera_overrides.is file has license limitation, users MUST consult ArduCAM and their own legal department.

<3> Run below command to start camera preview

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# export DISPLAY=:0
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# gst-launch-1.0 nvarguscamerasrc ! 'video/x-raw(memory:NVMM), width=(int)1920, height=(int)1080, \
                                       format=(string)NV12, framerate=(fraction)30/1' ! nvvidconv ! nvegltransform ! queue ! nveglglessink -e

7.5.2 Video decode
------------------

Deploy a mp4 video clip onto board, and then run below command:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# gst-launch-1.0 filesrc location=<filename.mp4> ! decodebin name=dec ! nvvidconv ! nvegltransform ! nveglglessink

7.5.3 Video encode
------------------

<1> Capture data from camera and encode with below command:

Only record data:
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# gst-launch-1.0 nvarguscamerasrc ! \
                                       'video/x-raw(memory:NVMM), width=(int)1920, height=(int)1080, \
                                       format=(string)NV12, framerate=(fraction)30/1' ! nvvidconv ! \
                                       'video/x-raw(memory:NVMM), format=I420' ! nvv4l2h264enc ! \
                                       h264parse ! qtmux ! filesink location=output.mp4 -e

Record data and preview:
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# gst-launch-1.0 nvarguscamerasrc ! tee name=t ! \
                                       'video/x-raw(memory:NVMM), width=(int)1920, height=(int)1080, \
                                       format=(string)NV12, framerate=(fraction)30/1' ! queue ! nvvideoconvert ! nvegltransform ! nveglglessink t. ! queue ! nvvidconv ! \
                                       'video/x-raw(memory:NVMM), format=I420' ! nvv4l2h264enc ! \
                                       h264parse ! qtmux ! filesink location=output.mp4 -e

<2> Play the recorded video clip with below command:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# gst-launch-1.0 filesrc location=./output.mp4 ! decodebin name=dec ! nvvidconv ! nvegltransform ! nveglglessink

7.5.4 Audio decode
------------------

If users want to verify audio feature of NVIDIA Orin platform, please connect a monitor that supports for sound playback function.

<1> Play audio encoded with ACC from a mp4 file with below command:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# gst-launch-1.0 filesrc location=<filename.mp4> ! qtdemux name=demux demux.audio_0 ! queue ! avdec_aac ! audioconvert ! alsasink -e

<2> Play a mp3 file with below command:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# gst-launch-1.0 filesrc location=<filename.mp3> ! mpegaudioparse ! avdec_mp3 ! audioconvert ! alsasink -e

7.5.5 Audio and Video decode
----------------------------

Play audio and video from a mp4 file at the same time with below command:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# gst-launch-1.0 filesrc location=<filename.mp4> ! decodebin name=decoder decoder. \
                                       ! queue ! nvvidconv ! nvegltransform ! nveglglessink decoder. ! queue ! audioconvert ! alsasink

7.6 DeepStream
--------------

7.6.1 DeepStream Introduction
-----------------------------

DeepStream is a streaming analytic toolkit to build AI-powered applications.

DeepStream takes the streaming data as input, from USB/CSI camera, video from file or streams over RTSP, and uses AI
and computer vision to generate insights from pixels for better understanding the content of data stream.

DeepStream consists of several hardware accelerator plugins that use accelerators such as VIC, GPU, DLA, NVDEC and NVENC.

DeepStream builds on top of several NVIDIA libraries from the CUDA-X stack such as CUDA, TensorRT, NVIDIA® Triton™ Inference
server and multimedia libraries. TensorRT accelerates the AI inference on NVIDIA GPU.

7.6.2 DeepStream Demonstration
------------------------------

There will be 3 DeepStream demo applications, and they use the traffic video clips as input source.
Based on DeepStream feature, the application can analyze the content of video clip, it can detect persons, cars, bikes, the type of cars,
the brand of cars, the number of cars and the color of cars. The DeepStream feature is based on CUDA，TensorRT and Multimedia.
So, if the DeepStream applications can work fine, it can prove the CUDA and Deep Learning features also work fine.

<1> DeepStream Application-1

It is a simple example that uses DeepStream element to detect cars, persons, and bikes on a given single H.264 stream.
Run DeepStream Application-1 with below commands:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# export DISPLAY=:0
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# cd /opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream-7.1/sources/apps/sample_apps/deepstream-test1
root@nvidia-orin-agx:/opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream-7.1/sources/apps/sample_apps/deepstream-test1# \
                          deepstream-test1-app /opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream-7.1/samples/streams/sample_720p.h264

At the first time of running DeepStream application, it needs spend some time to create engine from model files.
When the DeepStream application starts executing, users can see that there are red color rectangle around the detected objects.
Because the DeepStream find out persons, cars and bikes from the traffic video clip.

<2> DeepStream Application-2

The second DeepStream application is more powerful than the first one.
Run DeepStream Application-2 with below commands:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# export DISPLAY=:0
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# cd /opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream-7.1/sources/apps/sample_apps/deepstream-test2
root@nvidia-orin-agx:/opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream-7.1/sources/apps/sample_apps/deepstream-test2# \
                          deepstream-test2-app /opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream-7.1/samples/streams/sample_720p.h264

At the first time of running DeepStream application, it needs spend some time to create engine from model files.
When the DeepStream application starts executing, user can see there are also red color rectangle around the detected objects.
Besides detect the person, cars, and bikes. User also can see there are some strings on the top red rectangle, it indicates
the color, the brand, the type and the number of cars, such as the color is silvery, the brand is Toyota, Mercedes Benz or Honda,
the type is SUV.

<3> DeepStream Application-3

The third application show DeepStream can accept more than one video streams as input source. It can analyze multiple video
streams at the same time, and show them with Multiview style on the screen.
Run DeepStream Application-3 with below commands:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# export DISPLAY=:0
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# cd /opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream-7.1/sources/apps/sample_apps/deepstream-test3
root@nvidia-orin-agx:/opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream-7.1/sources/apps/sample_apps/deepstream-test3# \
                          deepstream-test3-app file:///opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream-7.1/samples/streams/sample_1080p_h264.mp4 \
                          file:///opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream-7.1/samples/streams/sample_720p.mp4

Note: All the above DeepStream demonstrations need DP or HDMI monitor as output end, so please connect a kind of monitor before
      starting your NVIDIA Orin platform.

7.7 NVIDIA Container Toolkit
----------------------------

7.7.1 Introduction
------------------

NVIDIA Container Toolkit is a collection of libraries and utilities that enables users to build and run GPU-accelerated containers.
It essentially provides a way to expose NVIDIA GPUs to Linux containers, allowing them to be used for tasks like deep learning model
training or other computationally intensive operations.

7.7.2 Container Demonstration
-----------------------------

In WRLinux NVIDIA Orin BSP, nvidia-docker and nvidia-container-toolkit packages are integrated by default.
User can run NVIDIA official container with belwo steps.

<1> Set correct system time

Open timesyncd configuration file with below command:
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# vim /etc/systemd/timesyncd.conf

Add a reasonable NTP server, and then restart systemd-timesyncd service with below command:
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# systemctl restart systemd-timesyncd.service

And then check time sync status with below command:
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# timedatectl timesync-status
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# date

<2> Network Access Permissions(Optional)

Users needs to make sure they can access Internet smoothly. Maybe a root certificate authority that is suitable for
their network environment needs to be installed.
In addition, a network agent may be also an essential configuration to access Internet.

<3> Restart docker service

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# systemctl restart docker
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# systemctl status docker

<4> Pull NVIDIA container

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# docker pull nvcr.io/nvidia/deepstream-l4t:7.1-triton-multiarch

Users also can pull other containers from below link:
https://catalog.ngc.nvidia.com/orgs/nvidia/containers/deepstream-l4t

<5> Run NVIDIA container

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# export DISPLAY=:0

Allow external applications to connect to the host's X display
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# xhost +

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# docker run -it --rm --network=host --runtime nvidia -e DISPLAY=$DISPLAY -w /opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream-7.1 \
                                   -v /tmp/.X11-unix/:/tmp/.X11-unix nvcr.io/nvidia/deepstream-l4t:7.1-triton-multiarch

<6> Run DeepStream demo in container

0;root@nvidia-orin-agx: /opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream-7.1root@nvidia-orin-agx:/opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream-7.1# \
                        cd /opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream-7.1/sources/apps/sample_apps/deepstream-test1
0;root@nvidia-orin-agx: /opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream-7.1/sources/apps/sample_apps/deepstream-test1root@nvidia-orin-agx:\
                        /opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream-7.1/sources/apps/sample_apps/deepstream-test1# deepstream-test1-app \
                        /opt/nvidia/deepstream/deepstream-7.1/samples/streams/sample_720p.h264

Note: NFS is not able to serve as the backing filesystem, so users need to deploy WRLinux system on eMMC if they want to run container image.

7.8 CAN bus
-----------

7.8.1 CAN bus connection layout
-------------------------------

NVIDIA Orin platform supports CAN bus, but it needs external transceiver to implement data communication.
NVIDIA recommends the WaveShare SN65HVD230 CAN transceiver module for NVIDIA Orin platform, link as below:
https://www.amazon.com/SN65HVD230-CAN-Board-Communication-Development/dp/B00KM6XMXO

For NVIDIA Orin AGX platform:
There are 2 CAN ports in the 40-pin header(J30), so it needs 2 transceivers to connect the 2 CAN ports.
Detailed connection layout as below:

        CAN0 port                 transceiver 0             transceiver 1               CAN1 port
------------------------        -----------------         -----------------       -----------------------
|                       |       |               |         |               |       |                      |
|              CAN0_DIN |<------| RXD           |         |           RXD |------>| CAN1_DIN             |
|        (pin29 of J30) |       |         CAN H |<------->| CAN H         |       | (pin37 of J30)       |
|                       |       |               |         |               |       |                      |
|             CAN0_DOUT |------>| TXD           |         |           TXD |<------| CAN1_DOUT            |
|        (pin31 of J30) |       |               |         |               |       | (pin33 of J30)       |
|                       |       |               |         |               |       |                      |
|                   3V3 |------>| 3V3           |         |           3V3 |<------| 3V3                  |
|        (pin01 of J30) |       |         CAN L |<------->| CAN L         |       | (pin17 of J30)       |
|                       |       |               |         |               |       |                      |
|                   GND |------>| GND           |         |           GND |<------| GND                  |
|        (pin09 of J30) |       |               |         |               |       | (pin06 of J30)       |
|                       |       |               |         |               |       |                      |
------------------------        -----------------         -----------------       -----------------------

7.8.2 CAN bus data communication
--------------------------------

<1> Set pinmux CAN ports with below commands:

Set CAN0 In pin
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# devmem2 0x0c303018
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# devmem2 0x0c303018 h 0xc458

Set CAN0 Out pin
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# devmem2 0x0c303010
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# devmem2 0x0c303010 h 0xc400

Set CAN1 In pin
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# devmem2 0x0c303008
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# devmem2 0x0c303008 h 0xc458

Set CAN1 Out pin
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# devmem2 0x0c303000
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# devmem2 0x0c303000 h 0xc400

<2> Reload CAN driver below commands:
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# modprobe -r mttcan
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# modprobe mttcan

<3> Setup can0 and can1 with below command:
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# ip link set can0 up type can bitrate 500000 dbitrate 1000000 berr-reporting on fd on
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# ip link set can1 up type can bitrate 500000 dbitrate 1000000 berr-reporting on fd on
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# ifconfig

<4> Data send(can1) and receive(can0)
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# candump can0 &

Send a frame to can1 with CAN ID 123 and data "abcdabcd"
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# cansend can1 123#abcdabcd

Send a FD frame to can1 with CAN ID 123, flag bit 1, and data "abcdabcd"
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# cansend can1 123##1abcdabcd

Use cangen tool to generate CAN frame
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# cangen can1

<5> Check data sending and receiving on can0 and can1 with below command:
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# ip -details -statistics link show can0
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# ip -details -statistics link show can1

7.9 WIFI/BT
-----------

For NVIDIA Orin AGX platform:
It is installed a WIFI/BT module as below:
AW-CB375NF Dual-Band Wireless NIC, 2.4G/5GHz Dual-Band WiFi 5, RTL8822CE-CG Core, Bluetooth 5.0, Supports BLE
Introduction link: https://www.waveshare.com/wireless-aw-cb375nf.htm

7.9.1 WIFI Demonstration
------------------------

<1> Setup WIFI module with below command:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# ifconfig wlP1p1s0 up
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# iw wlP1p1s0 scan
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# iw dev wlP1p1s0 connect <AP essid>
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# iw dev wlP1p1s0 link
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# dhcpcd wlP1p1s0

<2> Ping AP with below command:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# ping <AP address> -I wlP1p1s0 -c 5

7.9.2 BT Demonstration
----------------------

<1> Setup BT module with below command:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# hciconfig hci0 up
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# hcitool -i hci0 scan
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# hcitool cc <BT MAC address>
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# hcitool con

<2> Ping remote BT device with below command:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# l2ping -c 5 <BT MAC address>

7.10 USB
--------

7.10.1 USB Host
---------------

For NVIDIA Orin AGX platform:
There are 4 USB Type-A ports(J33) on NVIDIA Orin AGX board, they all work in USB host mode.
Users can connect USB disk, mouse, keyboard and other USB device to any of them.

7.10.2 USB OTG
--------------

For NVIDIA Orin AGX platform:
There is one USB Type-C port(J40) on NVIDIA Orin AGX board, it works in USB otg mode.

<1> Work as USB host mode

When users connect USB disk, mouse, keyboard and other USB device with the USB Type-C port, these USB
device is able to be detected normally.

<2> Work as USB device mode

When users connect the USB Type-C port with Host PC, it can work in USB device mode.
For example, NVIDIA Orin board can work as USB disk.
Insert a USB disk into USB Type-A port of NVIDIA Orin board, and then run below command:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# modprobe g_mass_storage file=/dev/sdaX stall=0 removable=1

After running above command, there will be an USB disk detected on Host PC.

Note: Set a reasonable number for 'X' to use expected USB partition.

7.11 microSD card
-----------------

For NVIDIA Orin AGX platform:
There is a microSD card slot(J10) on the NVIDIA Orin AGX board.
When user insert a microSD card into slot, it will be detected, detailed output as below:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~#
mmc1: new ultra high speed SDR104 SDHC card at address 5048
mmcblk1: mmc1:5048 SD32G 28.8 GiB
 mmcblk1: p1 p2
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# ls /dev/mmcblk1*
/dev/mmcblk1  /dev/mmcblk1p1  /dev/mmcblk1p2

7.12 NVME card
--------------

For NVIDIA Orin AGX platform:
There is a M.2 M-Key slot(J11) on the NVIDIA Orin AGX board.
When user insert a NVME card into slot, it will be detected after system boots up, detailed output as below:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# ls /dev/nvme0*
/dev/nvme0  /dev/nvme0n1  /dev/nvme0n1p1

7.13 PCIe
---------

For NVIDIA Orin AGX platform:
There is a PCIe x16 connector(J6) on the NVIDIA Orin AGX board.

User can verify PCIe feature with below steps:

<1> Connect a PCIe network card with PCIe x16 connector(J6). For example, Intel Corporation 82574L Gigabit Network card.

<2> Add below kernel feature in local.conf
	KERNEL_FEATURES:append = " features/intel-e1xxxx/intel-e1xxxx.scc"

<3> Build and deploy WRLinux system referring to section 4 and section 5.

<4> After system boots up, verify PCIe feature with below command:

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# lspci
0005:00:00.0 PCI bridge: NVIDIA Corporation Device 229a (rev a1)
0005:01:00.0 Ethernet controller: Intel Corporation 82574L Gigabit Network Connection

root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# ifconfig
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# ethtool <PCIe network card interface name>
root@nvidia-orin-agx:~# ping <Remote PC IP address> -I <PCIe network card interface name> -c 5

7.14 OP-TEE
-----------

7.14.1 Building OP-TEE OS
-------------------------

Currently, OP-TEE feature is supported by default. When users build BSP project referring to section 4,
the optee-os(BL32 image) is built automatically, and can be deployed into system referring to section 5.

7.14.2 OP-TEE Verificaton
-------------------------

Run below command to validate the OP-TEE feature with highest level 15

	# xtest -l 15

	Theoretically, all the test cases should be passed, and users will get below test result.

	55826 subtests of which 0 failed
	137 test cases of which 0 failed
	0 test cases were skipped
	TEE test application done!


